  "summary": "A propeller pump adds energy almost entirely by the lift of its blades, like a ship propeller working in a tube: liquid passes straight through axially. Straightening vanes above the propeller remove the swirl and recover pressure. It delivers very large flows at low heads with high specific speed; the head–flow curve is steep and power rises toward shut-off, so these pumps must not be started or run against a closed valve.",
  "classification": {
    "family": "Rotodynamic, axial flow",
    "configuration": "Vertical wet pit, single stage, line-shaft"
  },
  "model_configuration": {
    "propeller": "4 blades, fixed pitch (adjustable/variable-pitch hubs are used on large units)",
    "diffuser": "7 straightening vanes",
    "specific_speed_note": "Axial pumps typically Ns > ~9,000 (US) / nq > ~160; verify."
  },
